.TH COMBINE 1.SH NAMEcombine \- interleave two files byte by byte.SH SYNOPSIS\fBcombine\fReven odd outfile [\-q].SH DESCRIPTION\fBcombine\fRwrites\fIoutfile\fRfrom two inputs, taking the first byte from\fIeven\fR,\fRthe next from\fIodd\fR,\fRand so on.  It is how a 16-bit EPROM image is put back together from thetwo 8-bit halves that were burned into a pair of chips.  It reports eachfile it opens and the number of bytes processed..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-q\fRquiet: no reports.  It goes after the three file names; in front of themit is taken as a request for the usage message..SH BUGSIt writes the final pair of bytes twice, so the output is two byteslonger than the inputs together: `ACEG' and `BDFH' give\fBABCDEFGHGH\fR.\fRCut it back to the right length with\fBtrunc\fR.\fR.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOtrunc, split